@page "/projects/DotnetEnvironmentProfiles"

<PageTitle>Dotnet.EnvironmentProfiles</PageTitle>

<div class="content">
    <div>
        <h1><b>Dotnet.EnvironmentProfiles</b></h1>
        <p>
            Dotnet.EnvironmentProfiles is a lightweight .NET library that adds deterministic, enterprise-friendly
            environment mapping on top of ASP.NET Core without replacing any built-in primitives. It introduces
            Environment Profiles, which map real-world SDLC environment names (QA, UAT, PreProd, and more) to the
            canonical runtime behaviors that ASP.NET Core expects (Development, Staging, Production).
        </p>
        <p>
            This keeps your actual environment names intact while ensuring application behavior (middleware, safety
            checks, config loading) stays aligned with canonical .NET semantics.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div>
        <h2>Why It Exists</h2>
        <p>ASP.NET Core recognizes only three canonical environment names:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Development</li>
            <li>Staging</li>
            <li>Production</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Enterprise teams rarely use just these. Common environment names include QA, UAT, CDE, PreProd, Perf,
            and others. Those names are typically set via ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT, but ASP.NET Core behavior still
            depends on the canonical three.
        </p>
        <p>
            Dotnet.EnvironmentProfiles bridges this gap without forcing you to rename environments or override
            ASP.NET Core internals.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div>
        <h2>What It Does</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Defines Environment Profiles as immutable, explicit objects that represent your real environment names.</li>
            <li>Maps every environment name to a canonical runtime behavior.</li>
            <li>Resolves environment names through a single source of truth (IEnvironmentProfileResolver).</li>
            <li>Provides thin extension methods for convenience without embedding rules in application code.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div>
        <h2>Core Concepts</h2>
        <h3>1) Environment Profile (Immutable)</h3>
        <p>
            A profile includes a Name (example: "QA") and a CanonicalEnvironment (Development, Staging, or Production).
            Profiles are explicit and immutable, making them safe for audits and predictable in production.
        </p>
        <p><code>EnvironmentProfile("QA", CanonicalEnvironment.Staging)</code></p>

        <h3>2) Default Profiles (Opinionated, Overridable)</h3>
        <p>The package ships with sensible defaults that are easy to override via DI:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Development -> Development</li>
            <li>Local -> Development</li>
            <li>Staging -> Staging</li>
            <li>QA -> Staging</li>
            <li>UAT -> Staging</li>
            <li>CDE -> Staging</li>
            <li>PreProd -> Staging</li>
            <li>Production -> Production</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>3) Resolver (Single Source of Truth)</h3>
        <p>All logic runs through <code>IEnvironmentProfileResolver</code>.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Accept raw environment name (usually from ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT).</li>
            <li>Resolve to an EnvironmentProfile.</li>
            <li>Fail fast or fallback deterministically for unknown values.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>4) Thin Extensions (No Hidden Logic)</h3>
        <p>Extensions exist only for convenience and always rely on the resolver:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>IsQa()</li>
            <li>IsUat()</li>
            <li>IsNonProduction()</li>
            <li>GetEnvironmentProfile()</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div>
        <h2>Usage Flow</h2>
        <ol>
            <li>ASP.NET Core reads ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT.</li>
            <li>App registers Dotnet.EnvironmentProfiles.</li>
            <li>Resolver maps name to profile.</li>
            <li>App asks questions like "Is this non-prod?"</li>
            <li>ASP.NET Core continues normally.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div>
        <h2>Example Usage</h2>
        <pre><code>services.AddEnvironmentProfiles(options =>
{
    options.SetProfile(new EnvironmentProfile("Perf", CanonicalEnvironment.Staging));
    options.UnknownEnvironmentBehavior = UnknownEnvironmentBehavior.UseFallbackCanonicalEnvironment;
    options.FallbackCanonicalEnvironment = CanonicalEnvironment.Production;
});

var resolver = provider.GetRequiredService&lt;IEnvironmentProfileResolver&gt;();
var profile = resolver.Resolve("QA");
var isNonProd = profile.IsNonProduction();</code></pre>
    </div>

    <div>
        <h2>Non-Goals (By Design)</h2>
        <p>This library does not:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Replace IHostEnvironment.</li>
            <li>Change ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT.</li>
            <li>Introduce new ASP.NET Core lifecycles.</li>
            <li>Override configuration loading behavior.</li>
            <li>Infer environment names magically.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>It is designed to be deterministic, transparent, and safe for production.</p>
    </div>

    <div>
        <h2>Where It Fits</h2>
        <p>Works across:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>ASP.NET Core web apps</li>
            <li>Worker services</li>
            <li>Console apps</li>
            <li>Functions and background services</li>
            <li>Anything that uses environment names can use profiles</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div>
        <h2>Current Status</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Pre-release (v0.2.0)</li>
